struct ToolbarTelemetry {
    let gleanWrapper: GleanWrapper

    init(gleanWrapper: GleanWrapper = DefaultGleanWrapper()) {
        self.gleanWrapper = gleanWrapper
    }

@MainActor
private func cancelEditMode(windowUUID: WindowUUID) {
    var url = tabManager(for: windowUUID).selectedTab?.url
    if let currentURL = url {
        url = (currentURL.isWebPage() || currentURL.isReaderModeURL) ? url : nil
    }

private func recordReaderModeTelemetry(state: AppState, windowUUID: WindowUUID) {
    guard let toolbarState = state.screenState(ToolbarState.self, for: .toolbar, window: windowUUID) else { return }

    let isReaderModeEnabled = switch toolbarState.addressToolbar.readerModeState {
    case .available: false // will be enabled after action gets executed
    default: true
    }

    toolbarTelemetry.readerModeButtonTapped(isPrivate: toolbarState.isPrivateMode, isEnabled: isReaderModeEnabled)
}

@MainActor
private static func handleShowReaderModeAction(state: BrowserViewControllerState,
                                               action: GeneralBrowserAction) -> BrowserViewControllerState {
    return BrowserViewControllerState(
        searchScreenState: state.searchScreenState,
        toast: state.toast,
        windowUUID: state.windowUUID,
        browserViewType: state.browserViewType,
        displayView: .readerModeLongPressAction,
        microsurveyState: MicrosurveyPromptState.reducer(state.microsurveyState, action))
}

findings.body:
`handleShowReaderModeAction` used to set `displayView: .readerMode`, which `BrowserViewController.handleDisplayActions` routes to `toggleReaderMode()`. This PR changes it to `.readerModeLongPressAction`, which is routed to `toggleReaderModeLongPressAction()`. That function creates a reading-list record for the current page and posts an announcement.

`showReaderMode` is dispatched by the reader-mode toolbar button tap (`ToolbarMiddleware.swift`, `.readerMode` case in `didTapButton`) and by the main menu (`MainMenuDetailState.swift`). So a plain tap on the reader-mode button now saves the page to reading list and never enables or disables reader mode. The main menu's reader-mode item is affected the same way.

The `.readerMode` display state now has no producers, and the BVC switch case for it is dead code. The PR's own commit did not add a test, and the existing `ToolbarMiddlewareTests` only check the dispatched action, not the reducer output.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/BrowserViewController/State/BrowserViewControllerState.swift" lines="563-569"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The long-press path (`addToReadingListLongPressAction`) already mapped to `.readerModeLongPressAction` before this PR, so the add-to-reading-list behavior was correct. The regression is limited to the `showReaderMode` tap.

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/BrowserViewController/State/BrowserViewControllerState.swift" lines="566"}
            displayView: .readerMode,

`recordReaderModeTelemetry` runs before the `showReaderMode` action is dispatched, so the `readerModeState` it reads is the pre-toggle state. When the state is `.available`, a tap enables reader mode, so `isEnabled` should be `true`. This PR changed it to `false`, and `default` (`.active`, which disables reader mode) now reports `true`. Every reader-mode tap is now logged with the opposite value. The inline comment ("will be enabled after action gets executed") describes the old, correct mapping.

The PR gives no reason for flipping this. There is no test for `readerModeButtonTapped`, so the inversion went unnoticed. Glean data for this event will be wrong from release onward.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/Toolbars/Redux/ToolbarMiddleware.swift" lines="521-528"}

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/Toolbars/Redux/ToolbarMiddleware.swift" lines="524-526"}
        case .available: true // will be enabled after action gets executed
        default: false

`cancelEditMode` used to clear the URL for reader-mode pages: `url = (isWebPage && !isReaderModeURL) ? url : nil`. This PR changes the operator to `||`, so reader-mode URLs are now kept in the `ToolbarAction(cancelEdit)` payload while the original intent was to hide them. The change is not related to QR removal, it is not mentioned in the PR description, and no test covers it.

It may be deliberate, but as written this silently changes what the address bar shows after cancelling edit on a reader page. If it is intentional, it needs its own commit or PR description. If not, revert it.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/Toolbars/Redux/ToolbarMiddleware.swift" lines="482-488"}

The `qr_code.scanned` counter and the `TelemetryWrapper` mapping that fed it were removed. In this codebase they were only recorded from the BVC delegate methods that this PR deletes. The QR scanner still exists and is still reachable from FxA sign-in, onboarding, settings, and remote tabs through `QRCodeNavigationHandler`. `FirefoxAccountSignInViewController.didScanQRCodeWithURL` does not record this counter, so scans in those flows now go unrecorded.

This is a data-collection change, not just dead-code cleanup. The probe removal needs a data review, and the data stewards should be told that the `qr_code.scanned` series will drop to zero rather than just stop being reported from BVC.

